s":[{"value":"Columbia U.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/902749"}},{"value":"ISCAP, New York","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/908464"}}],"signature_block":"WANGj","curated_relation":true}],"curated":true,"figures":[{"key":"77d3028dfa16fa8aa9cc7155eab9b47b","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/77d3028dfa16fa8aa9cc7155eab9b47b","source":"arxiv","caption":"\\em Feynman diagram representation of eq.~(\\ref{Dyson series}): the solid lines represent the $\\phi$ propagators and the gray circles the two-point function of $\\mathcal{O}$.","filename":"DysonSeries.png"}],"license":[{"url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","imposing":"arXiv"}],"texkeys":["Endlich:2012vt"],"citeable":true,"imprints":[{"date":"2013-11-04"}],"keywords":[{"value":"47.75.+f","schema":"PACS"},{"value":"11.10.Ef","schema":"PACS"},{"value":"expansion: derivative","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"frequency: low","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"effective field theory","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"dissipation","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"hydrodynamics","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"viscosity","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"fluid","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"charge: conservation law","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"perturbation","schema":"INSPIRE"}],"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"We introduce dissipative effects in the effective field theory of hydrodynamics. We do this in a model-independent fashion by coupling the long-distance degrees of freedom explicitly kept in the effective field theory to a generic sector that “lives in the fluid,” which corresponds physically to the microscopic constituents of the fluid. At linear order in perturbations, the symmetries, the derivative expansion, and the assumption that this microscopic sector is thermalized allow us to characterize the leading dissipative effects at low frequencies via three parameters only, which correspond to bulk viscosity, shear viscosity, and—in the presence of a conserved charge—heat conduction. Using our methods we rederive the Kubo relations for these transport coefficients.","source":"APS"},{"value":"We introduce dissipative effects in the effective field theory of hydrodynamics. We do this in a model-independent fashion by coupling the long-distance degrees of freedom explicitly kept in the effective field theory to a generic sector that \"lives in the fluid\", which corresponds physically to the microscopic constituents of the fluid. At linear order in perturbations, the symmetries, the derivative expansion, and the assumption that this microscopic sector is thermalized, allow us to characterize the leading dissipative effects at low frequencies via three parameters only, which correspond to bulk viscosity, shear viscosity, and--in the presence of a conserved charge--heat conduction. Using our methods we re-derive the Kubo relations for these transport coefficients.","source":"arXiv"}],"references":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/700964"},"reference":{"misc":["[arXiv:"],"label":"1","title":{"title":"Null energy condition and superluminal propagation"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Dubovsky, S."},{"full_name":"Gregoire, T."},{"full_name":"Nicolis, A."},{"full_name":"Rattazzi, R."}],"arxiv_eprint":"hep-th/0512260","publication_info":{"year":2006,"artid":"025","page_start":"025","journal_title":"JHEP","journal_volume":"03"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/878602"},"reference":{"label":"2","title":{"title":"The quantum mechanics of perfect fluids"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Endlich, S."},{"full_name":"Nicolis, A."},{"full_name":"Rattazzi, R."},{"full_name":"Wang, J."}],"arxiv_eprint":"1011.6396","publication_info":{"year":2011,"artid":"102","page_start":"102","journal_title":"JHEP","journal_volume":"04"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/892384"},"reference":{"label":"3","title":{"title":"Hall Viscosity f